❯ Should an OrcID be considered an instance of schemaPerson?                                                                                                                                                                         
● No. An ORCID identifies a person, but it is not itself a person. The ORCID URI (https://orcid.org/0000-...) serves as a persistent identifier for a person, not as an instance of schema:Person.

  In the current contributors.ttl, the mlips: URIs are the schema:Person instances, and vivo:orcidId links them to their ORCID URIs. This is correct.

  An alternative approach is to use the ORCID URI directly as the person's URI, eliminating the need for a separate property:

  <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7896-0875> a schema:Person ;
      schema:familyName "Hernandez"@en ;
      schema:givenName "Daniel"@en .

  This is valid and common in linked data — the ORCID URI then serves double duty as both the identifier and the subject. The tradeoff is that your data becomes dependent on the ORCID namespace.

  The current approach (separate mlips: URIs linked to ORCIDs via vivo:orcidId) gives you more flexibility — a person without an ORCID can still have a URI, and you can link to multiple identifier systems.

❯ What is the class of an ORCID identifier?                                                                                                                                                                                          
● There isn't a widely adopted OWL class specifically for "an ORCID identifier" in standard vocabularies. However, there are a few ways to type it:

  - datacite:PersonalIdentifier — from the DataCite ontology, used in scholarly metadata
  - schema:PropertyValue — Schema.org's generic approach for structured identifiers (used by Google Scholar, DBLP, etc.):

  mlips:Hernandez a schema:Person ;
      schema:identifier [
          a schema:PropertyValue ;
          schema:propertyID "ORCID" ;
          schema:value "0000-0002-7896-0875"
      ] .

  - adms:Identifier — from the EU's Asset Description Metadata Schema

  In practice, most linked data projects don't assign a class to the ORCID URI itself — they simply treat it as a named individual (an opaque URI) and let vivo:orcidId convey the semantics. The ORCID URI
  <https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7896-0875> is just "a thing that identifies a person," and the property already tells you it's an ORCID.

  If you want to be explicit, you could declare it in your own ontology:

  mlips:OrcidIdentifier a owl:Class ;
      rdfs:subClassOf schema:PropertyValue ;
      rdfs:comment "An ORCID persistent identifier for a researcher." .

  But this is rarely necessary — vivo:orcidId already implies the range is an ORCID.

