  │         Tool         │                                                Notes                                                │
  ├──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ YASGUI               │ Best general-purpose choice. Client-side, works with any endpoint                                   │
  ├──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Oxigraph built-in UI │ Oxigraph server ships with a basic SPARQL editor already — could be enough for Phase 1              │
  ├──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Sparklis             │ Natural language-like SPARQL builder — interesting for non-technical researchers, but less flexible │
  └──────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  My recommendation

  Start with YASGUI — it's the path of least resistance, requires no extra backend, and your Axum app just needs to proxy or expose the Oxigraph SPARQL endpoint. You can customize it later with saved queries, per-user query
  history, etc.

